3. The specification, and the test corpus
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Neither is part of the program, and neither is in the binary. They are recorded here because a
reader of this repository should know their terms — and, for the first, because their absence is
deliberate and needs explaining.

doc/*.pdf and doc/md/* are ISO 32000-2, the ISO/TS extensions to it, and the PDF Association's
application notes and guides. This project is written against them: the citation checker and the
conformance ledger read the Markdown conversion, and several tests and measurement examples open
the PDF. They are ISO's and the PDF Association's, free to obtain from the PDF Association at
https://pdfa.org/ and NOT free to pass on — a repository carrying them in the clear would be
passing them on to everyone who clones it.

So they are not carried in the clear. doc/specifications.zip holds all twenty-eight files
encrypted; .gitignore keeps the unpacked copies untracked. Open it with

    unzip -P <password> doc/specifications.zip

from the workspace root, which puts them where the code looks. The password is not a security
measure and is not treated as one: it is what keeps a clone of this repository from being a
redistribution of ISO's text. Ask the project owner for it, or download your own copies from the
PDF Association under the file names the archive uses. CI reads it from a repository secret.

doc/md/ is docling's conversion of those PDFs (doc/README-script.txt), regenerable from them and
a derived work of the same documents, which is why it is inside the archive rather than beside it.

doc/pdf.js is a submodule holding Mozilla's pdf.js test corpus, Apache-2.0. It is used by the
gates and by nothing that ships.

Four more test corpora are submodules under doc/corpora/. A submodule is a pin rather than a
copy — this repository records a URL and a commit identifier, and a clone fetches the bytes from
their own server under their own terms — so none of them is redistributed here and none of these
lines is an obligation being discharged. They are here as a courtesy to the people who assembled
the files, and doc/third-party-data.md is the full reading.

    doc/corpora/pdf20examples   The PDF Association's ISO 32000-2 example files, CC BY-SA 4.0.
    doc/corpora/pdf-differences The PDF Association's reader-divergence files, Apache-2.0.
    doc/corpora/pdfbox          Apache PDFBox's test inputs and their frozen PDFTextStripper
                                output, Apache-2.0, with NOTICE.txt beside them in the checkout.
    doc/corpora/format-corpus   The Open Preservation Foundation's format corpus, three of its
                                PDF directories. pdfCabinetOfHorrors states CC0 outright;
                                pdf-handbuilt-test-corpus states nothing of its own and takes the
                                repository's CC0 default, crediting an iPRES 2017 paper and the
                                artefact deposited at DOI 10.22000/53; govdocs1-error-pdfs is
                                copied from Govdocs1, whose own terms call it freely
                                redistributable to the best of their knowledge and ask for this
                                citation: Garfinkel, Farrell, Roussev and Dinolt, Bringing
                                Science to Digital Forensics with Standardized Forensic Corpora,
                                DFRWS 2009, Montreal, Canada.
